\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Poverty Mapping in the Age of Machine Learning
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Paul Corral, Heath Henderson, Sandra Segovia, and Kevin McGee
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
Recent years have witnessed considerable methodological advances in poverty
 mapping, much of which has focused on the application of modern machine-learnin
g approaches to remotely-sensed data.
 Poverty maps produced with these methods generally share a common validation
 procedure, which assesses model performance by comparing sub-national poverty
 estimates with survey-based, direct estimates.
 While unbiased, direct estimates can be imprecise measures of true poverty
 rates, meaning that it is unclear whether the standard validation procedures
 are informative of actual model performance.
 In this paper, we examine the credibility of existing approaches to model
 validation by constructing a pseudo-census from the Mexican Intercensal
 Survey of 2015, which we use to conduct several design-based simulation
 experiments.
 We find that the standard validation procedure can be misleading in terms
 of model assessment, with notable implications for model selection.
 Further, our closest approximation to existing machine-learning approaches
 performs poorly when evaluated against 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

true
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 poverty rates and fails to outperform traditional poverty mapping methods
 in targeting simulations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Poverty maps provide granular estimates of poverty at the sub-national level
 in order to deepen the understanding of poverty in a given country, better
 inform the targeting of resources, and support the design of interventions
 tailored to local needs 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "bedi2007poverty"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 As household surveys are generally unrepresentative for small geographical
 areas, producing detailed poverty maps often requires using small area
 estimation, which consists of using statistical methods to predict poverty
 levels on the basis of household- or area-level characteristics.
 The basic procedure entails two steps: (1) using household survey data,
 fit a statistical model of some poverty measure (e.g., consumption or wealth)
 to select household- or area-level characteristics, and (2) apply the model
 to census data available for all households or geographical entities to
 estimate poverty at the sub-national level.
 At a minimum, the procedure thus requires both household survey and census
 data to produce a sufficiently detailed poverty map.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The literature on small area estimation is rich and several variations on
 this basic procedure have been proposed.
 Unit-level models conduct estimation and prediction at the household level,
 assuming a linear relationship between the poverty measure and household-level
 characteristics 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "hentschel1998combining,elbers2003micro,molina2010small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Unit-level models are not well-suited to situations where the survey and
 census data correspond to different years, which is often the case in developin
g countries where censuses are conducted infrequently.
 Area-level models represent a feasible alternative that similarly rely
 on linear functional forms, but conduct estimation and prediction using
 only aggregate data for the geographical entities of interest 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "fay1979estimates,torabi2014small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Unit-context models represent another alternative and are characterized
 by an estimation stage wherein household-level measures are modeled exclusively
 as a linear function of area-level characteristics 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "nguyen2012method,lange2018small,masaki2020small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recent developments in poverty mapping have focused on the application of
 machine learning to remotely-sensed data, largely in response to the issue
 of out-of-date census information.
 These approaches also use survey-derived measures of poverty, but are distingui
sed by a first stage where a machine-learning model is fit to remotely-sensed
 covariates (e.g., data derived from satellite imagery or call detail records)
 rather than census-based covariates.
 For example, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chi2022microestimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 matched data from several remotely-sensed data sources to survey-based
 measures of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

village
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 wealth for 56 countries, and then fit a prediction model using gradient
 boosting machines.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Their remotely-sensed data includes high-resolution satellite imagery, mobile
 phone data, topographic maps, and connectivity data from Facebook.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 They then applied the model to the populated surface of all 135 low- and
 middle-income countries to develop granular poverty maps for each country.
 Similar approaches have been used for individual countries, including Rwanda
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "blumenstock2015predicting"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, Senegal 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "pokhriyal2017combining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, Bangladesh 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "steele2017mapping"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, and Belize 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "hersh2021open"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, among others.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "jean2016combining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yeh2020using"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "lee2020high"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "aiken2022machine"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for examples of other recent applications.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Poverty maps produced with machine-learning methods generally share a common
 validation procedure, which can be summarized in the following steps: (1)
 using survey data calculate a poverty measure for the geographic unit of
 interest and merge the poverty measure with features from some remotely-sensed
 data source; (2) randomly split the dataset into training and testing subsample
s; (3) fit a machine-learning model to the training data and use the model
 to predict poverty in the testing data; (4) calculate the out-of-sample
 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 by regressing the observed poverty measures from the testing data on the
 predicted poverty measures for the same geographic units.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Rather than relying on a single training-testing split of the data, many
 studies use cross-validation and report the average 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 across various training-testing splits.
 For examples of the above procedure, see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "jean2016combining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pokhriyal2017combining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "steele2017mapping"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "lee2020high"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yeh2020using"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, or 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chi2022microestimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Note that a few studies use a similar procedure, but fit the machine-learning
 model to household-level data and then aggregate the model's predictions
 to the geographic unit of interest 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "blumenstock2015predicting,hersh2021open,aiken2022machine"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The poverty measures used in this procedure are most often direct, sample-based
 estimates rather than the true poverty measures for the regions of interest.
 While direct estimates are unbiased, they can be imprecise estimates of
 true poverty measures, meaning that it is unclear whether the validation
 procedure is informative of actual model performance 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "corral2021pull"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this paper, we seek to understand the reliability of the above validation
 procedure.
 Our approach consists of constructing a pseudo-census (hereafter 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

census
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) that we use to conduct a design-based simulation experiment.
 Our experiments entail repeatedly drawing survey samples from the census
 where each sample produces direct poverty estimates for the sampled geographic
 areas.
 For each direct estimate, the presence of the census means that we observe
 the true poverty measure corresponding to the direct estimate and we use
 these true poverty measures to gain insight not only into the credibility
 of model validation based on direct estimates, but also the performance
 of machine-learning methods when validated against the true poverty measures.
 Given that population censuses rarely collect detailed data on income or
 expenditures, we construct our census from a large-scale household survey
 conducted in Mexico: the Mexican Intercensal Survey of 2015.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Prior to presenting our simulation results, we discuss three ways in which
 the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 can be misleading in terms of model assessment.
 Specifically, we show analytically that the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 based on direct estimates is biased downward, that it is insensitive to
 differences in location and scale between the poverty estimates and the
 reference measures, and that it is context-dependent in the sense that
 it is influenced by the variance of the true poverty rates.
 We argue that the mean-squared error is a better measure for understanding
 the strength of the association between the poverty estimates and reference
 measures.
 In addition, we argue that the ultimate concern is to understand how different
 methodological choices affect poverty by influencing the efficiency of
 targeting.
 We therefore propose a targeting experiment through which we examine the
 relative ability of alternative mapping methods to alleviate poverty in
 the context of the Mexican data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The results of our simulations can be summarized as follows.
 First, we find that the magnitude of the downward bias of the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 based on direct estimates is large, with the true 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 being around 35 to 50 percent higher depending on the model considered.
 We further find that this bias has important implications for model selection
 in that the direct 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 incorrectly identifies the appropriate level of estimation in the vast
 majority of our simulations.
 Second, when assessing model performance based on the true poverty rates
 using the mean squared error, we find that our closest approximation to
 the standard machine-learning implementation performs poorly relative to
 several benchmark models.
 We find that these performance issues are largely due to the limited predictive
 power of remotely-sensed covariates relative to census-based covariates.
 Finally, our targeting simulations show that none of our machine-learning
 implementations outperform more traditional poverty mapping methods that
 are feasible with the same data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our paper builds on the work of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2021map"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, who similarly used the Mexican Intercensal Survey to examine the performance
 of several poverty mapping methods.
 The authors nevertheless focused exclusively on traditional poverty mapping
 approaches and did not consider the performance of machine-learning methods.
 Importantly, we are not the first paper to evaluate the performance of
 machine-learning methods relative to census data.
 While 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yeh2020using"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chi2022microestimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 validate their models relative to censuses, both papers use the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 as a performance metric and focus on wealth estimates rather than poverty.
 Our paper is thus distinct from this previous work in the sense that we
 evaluate the performance of machine learning for the estimation of poverty
 rather than wealth, and we also consider other performance metrics that
 are arguably better-suited for assessing the reliability of poverty maps.
\end_layout

