\begin_layout Standard
Recall from Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:validation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 that the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 measure often used to assess the performance of machine-learning models
 is biased downward.
 To understand the extent of this bias in practice, we have calculated the
 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 associated with the XGBoost model when using both the direct estimates
 and true poverty rates as the basis for performance assessment (i.e., based
 on Eqs.
\begin_inset Formula $\negthinspace$
\end_inset

 [
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:r2direct"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

] and [
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:r2true"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

], respectively).
 We conduct this comparison at two different levels of estimation, namely
 the PSU and municipality levels.
 All 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 calculations are nevertheless made at the municipality level, meaning that
 the PSU-level model is estimated at that level and then the poverty estimates
 are aggregated to the municipality level.
 For each of our 500 samples, we thus calculate four 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 measures: (1) PSU-level estimates evaluated against the direct poverty
 estimates, (2) PSU-level estimates evaluated against the true poverty rates,
 (3) municipality-level estimates evaluated against the direct poverty estimates
, and (4) municipality-level estimates evaluated against the true poverty
 rates.
 All models use only census-based covariates.
