\begin_layout Standard
In Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:validation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, we discussed two other ways that the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 can be misleading for assessing model performance: it is invariant to locationa
l shifts (see Eq.
 [
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:shift"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

]) and influenced by the variance of the true poverty rates (see Eq.
 [
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:variance"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

]).
 Using the Mexican data, we can assess the relevance of the former issue
 by comparing the true 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 to a constrained version of the true 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 that fixes the intercept and coefficient to zero and one, respectively.
 By constraining the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 in this manner, we remove its ability to adjust for systematic differences
 between the poverty estimates and the true poverty rates.
 Panel (b) of Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
