\begin_layout Standard
Given the shortcomings of the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

, we have argued that the MSE represents a better metric for model assessment.
 Panel (a) of Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:mse"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 thus presents our MSE results for several alternative models.
 The first three models apply XGBoost at the municipality level using three
 alternative covariate sets: only GIS-based covariates, only census-based
 covariates, and then a model with all available covariates.
 The fouth model is estimated at the PSU level and only uses census-based
 covariates.
 The final two models are traditional small-area models that we use to benchmark
 the performance of the XGBoost models.
 In particular, the empirical best (EB) model is often considered the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

gold standard
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of small-area estimation and corresponds to an ideal scenario where up-to-date,
 micro-level census data are available.
 The unit-context (UC) model is often considered the preferred model when
 census data are out-of-date and is thus a direct competitor to the machine-lear
ning models, which are often motivated by a lack of up-to-date census data.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recall from Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:validation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 that one advantage of the MSE is that it can be calculated for each geographica
l entity.
 As such, each box plot in panel (a) of Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:mse"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 summarizes the distribution of municipality-level MSE estimates rather
 than the aggregate MSE for each sample (i.e., the results are based on Eq.
 [
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:mseg"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

]).
 Turning to the results, the XGBoost model with GIS-based covariates is
 our closest approximation to the standard implementation of machine learning
 in the context of poverty mapping.
 This model is nevertheless the worst performing of all the models: the
 median MSE for the model with GIS-based covariates is 0.008 (with a root
 MSE of about 0.09), meaning that the median error is roughly nine percentage
 points.
 In contrast, the EB and UC models achieve median MSE levels of 0.003 and
 0.004, with corresponding root MSE values of 0.05 and 0.06, respectively.
 While the standard implementation shows relatively poor performance, the
 remaining three XGBoost models that incorporate census-based covariates
 perform much better, with the PSU-level model achieving a median MSE of
 0.002 and outperforming both of the traditional approaches.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The bias associated with poverty estimates is especially important due to
 the policy relevance of understanding absolute living standards.
 In panel (b) of Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:mse"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, we thus present box plots for the municipality-level bias estimates associated
 with each model (see Eq.
 [
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:mseg"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

] for the bias-variance decomposition of the MSE).
 That is, each box plot summarizes the distribution of bias estimates across
 all municipalities in our Mexican data.
 The median bias for each model is approximately zero, with the possible
 exception of the UC model, which exhibits a downward bias.
 The median bias of the UC model is -0.03, meaning that it underestimates
 municipality-level poverty rates by about three percentage points.
 We further find that the bias estimates for the XGBoost model with GIS-based
 covariates exhibits considerable variation, with minimum and maximum biases
 reaching -0.43 and 0.43, respectively.
 That is, the model with GIS-based covariates is severely biased for some
 municipalities, even though its median bias is approximately zero.
 The XGBoost models incorporating census-based covariates exhibit much less
 variation.
\end_layout

