\begin_layout Standard
Up to this point, we have focused exclusively on the performance of XGBoost,
 but there are nevertheless several other machine-learning models that might
 be used for poverty mapping.
 Leading alternatives include the least absolute shrinkage and selection
 operator (lasso) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "tibshirani1996"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, the random forest 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "breiman2001"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, and Bayesian additive regression trees (BART) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "chipman2010bart"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 While the lasso is a simple way to conduct variable selection and regularizatio
n in the context of linear regression, the random forest and BART models
 are quite similar to XGBoost in that they all rely on flexible regression
 tree frameworks.
 We ask whether these alternative models can perform better than XGBoost
 within the confines of the standard implementation, which relies exclusively
 on GIS-based covariates.
 Panels (a) and (b) of Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
