 thus compares our six initial models in terms of their ability to reduce
 poverty in the context of our targeting simulations.
 In these simulations, a hypothetical poverty-alleviation program targeted
 on the basis of true municipality-level poverty rates is able to reduce
 the overall poverty headcount to just under 20 percent.
 While the achieved poverty rates of all models fall within the 20 percent
 range, we see once again that the XGBoost model with GIS covariates is
 the worst performing model.
 The best performing XGBoost model is the implementation at the PSU level,
 which achieves a median poverty rate across all simulations of 20.02.
 Critically, even this model falls short of the UC benchmark, which achieves
 a median poverty rate of 20.01.
 Machine learning thus fails to outperform the traditional UC model, which
 is feasible with the same data.
